{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"MNIST Classification Task\"\n",
        "html:\n",
        "  theme: darkly\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "We will use the dataset from [OpenMl](https://www.openml.org/) which is a \n",
        "website with multiple ML datasets and API's at which you can use."
      ],
      "id": "440f059f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.datasets import fetch_openml\n",
        "\n",
        "mnist = fetch_openml(\"mnist_784\", as_frame=False)"
      ],
      "id": "603464c8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We say `as_frame=False` here because the mnist dataset contains images which \n",
        "isn't very dataframe compatible. We can instead work with images which are \n",
        "represented by bitmaps.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "#### `sklearn.datasets`\n",
        "\n",
        "There are mainly three types of functions available from this submodule:\n",
        "1. `fetch_*` - loads real-world datasets\n",
        "2. `load_*` - returns toy datasets\n",
        "3. `make_*` - returns (X,y) tuple of data\n"
      ],
      "id": "a10fbdc9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X,y = mnist.data, mnist.target\n",
        "\n",
        "print(X.shape)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(y.shape)"
      ],
      "id": "8679a13a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### How to view images"
      ],
      "id": "4bbb479d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "def plot_digit(image_data):\n",
        "  image = image_data.reshape(28,28)\n",
        "  plt.imshow(image, cmap=\"binary\")\n",
        "  plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "\n",
        "digit = X[0]\n",
        "plot_digit(digit)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "84619586",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### splitting into test and training sets\n",
        "\n",
        "The mnist data is already split into a train and test set which has been shuffled\n",
        "for us. This helps out since it **guarantees cross validation** will be similar"
      ],
      "id": "c8754932"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = X[:60000], X[60000:], y[:60000], y[60000:]"
      ],
      "id": "87f1d3b1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Training a Binary Classifier\n",
        "\n",
        "Before we do crazy shit by predicting the exact digit, let's simplify by just\n",
        "predicting a single digit. \n",
        "\n",
        "Our chosen digit will be 5.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is computationally less expensive and less effort than using a more complex\n",
        "model like a neural network. Our chosen model will be `Stochastic Gradient\n",
        "Descent`."
      ],
      "id": "cd96622a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "y_train_5 = (y_train == '5')\n",
        "y_test_5 = (y_test == '5')"
      ],
      "id": "8fc990a4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.linear_model import SGDClassifier\n",
        "\n",
        "sgd_clf = SGDClassifier(random_state=42)\n",
        "sgd_clf.fit(X_train, y_train_5)"
      ],
      "id": "c61bc7cc",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "sgd_clf.predict([digit])"
      ],
      "id": "73a6ca60",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Measuring the Model Performance\n",
        "\n",
        "Evaluating a classifier is trickier than a regressor and there are a bunch of\n",
        "concepts to understand.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Measuring Accuracy using Cross-Validation"
      ],
      "id": "06d6603d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\n",
        "\n",
        "cross_val_score(sgd_clf, X_train, y_train_5, cv=3, scoring=\"accuracy\")"
      ],
      "id": "60dae810",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This looks good as we have above 95% accuracy for all folds but we shouldn't \n",
        "get too excited by this just yet. First let's look at whether the `DummyClassifier`\n",
        "gives the same results."
      ],
      "id": "20fa9179"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.dummy import DummyClassifier\n",
        "\n",
        "dummy_clf = DummyClassifier()\n",
        "dummy_clf.fit(X_train, y_train_5)\n",
        "print(any(dummy_clf.predict(X_train)))"
      ],
      "id": "27717bc2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "No 5's are detected by our dummy classifier so let's check the \n",
        "CV score:"
      ],
      "id": "ff03b130"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "cross_val_score(dummy_clf, X_train, y_train_5, cv=3, scoring=\"accuracy\")"
      ],
      "id": "88e6bd8b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We now have 90% accuracy. This is simply becuase only 10% of the images are 5's\n",
        "so you have some imbalanced data which means you have a 90% chance of predicting \n",
        "not 5 and being correct.\n",
        "\n",
        "A better way to evaluate such skewed data is with a `Confusion Matrix`.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Confusion Matrices\n",
        "\n",
        "The main purpose of Confusion Matrices is to check the False Positives and False\n",
        "Negatives of a classifier compared to True Positives and -Negatives. "
      ],
      "id": "c4c8967e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_predict\n",
        "from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "y_train_pred = cross_val_predict(sgd_clf, X_train, y_train_5, cv=3)\n",
        "cm = confusion_matrix(y_train_5, y_train_pred)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(cm)"
      ],
      "id": "50bbd0cc",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This block here returns a predicted values list which still implements cross-\n",
        "validation which we use in our confusion matrix.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#tbl-confusion-matrix}\n",
        "|               | Predicted: Not 5 | Predicted: Is 5 |\n",
        "|---------------|------------------|-----------------|\n",
        "| **Actual: Not 5** | 53892            | 687             |\n",
        "| **Actual: Is 5**  | 1891             | 3530            |\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "From the confusion matrix, we can derive **precision and recall** metrics.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "precision = \\frac{TP}{TP+FP}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "alongside this, we calculate the **recall** rate which is also called the **sensitivity**\n",
        "and the **true positive rate**.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "recall = \\frac{TP}{TP+FN}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Scikit-Learn has metrics as well to predict precision and recall such as:"
      ],
      "id": "f3975ccc"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.metrics import precision_score, recall_score\n",
        "\n",
        "precision_score(y_train_5, y_train_pred)\n",
        "recall_score(y_train_5, y_train_pred)"
      ],
      "id": "2ae300ab",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Combining Precision and Recall \n",
        "\n",
        "Its often more convenient to combine the metricxs into a single more intepretable\n",
        "metric like the **F1-score** which is the *harmonic mean* between the two \n",
        "metrics:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align}\n",
        "F_1 =& \\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{precision}+\\frac{1}{recall}} \\\\\n",
        "=& 2 \\times \\frac{precision \\times recall}{precision \\times recall} \\\\\n",
        "=& \\frac{TP}{TP+\\frac{FN+TP}{2}}\n",
        "\\end{align}\n",
        "$$"
      ],
      "id": "e423ac9b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.metrics import f1_score\n",
        "\n",
        "f1_score(y_train_5, y_train_pred)"
      ],
      "id": "39ababb5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ">[!Tip] F1 isn't perfect\n",
        "> F1 score favors classifiers with similar precision and recall. Sometimes you \n",
        "> care more about one metric than another.\n",
        "\n",
        ">[!Example] Predicting Child Safe Videos\n",
        "> You would **care more about high precision** since rejecting a few good vidoes\n",
        "> are fine as long as no bad ones slips out, i.e. low recall\n",
        "\n",
        ">[!Example] Predicting Shoplifters\n",
        "> You would rather have **high recall** such that no false negatives slips out.\n",
        "> This would cause false alarms but that's better than the alternative\n",
        "\n",
        "Both of these examples shows the importance of understanindg the data you're \n",
        "working with.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Precision/Recall Trade-off\n",
        "\n",
        "[Precision-Recall Trade-off](Trade-off.png)\n",
        "\n",
        "Each instance is determined as either positive or negative by the decision \n",
        "function of the classifier. Increasing the threshold would increase recall \n",
        "but lower percision and vice versa.\n",
        "\n",
        "Using scikit-learn's `predict()` function for a classifier directly doesn't let\n",
        "you set the threshold but we can use the `decision_function()` of a classifier\n",
        "and set the threshold manually like this:"
      ],
      "id": "c008c56f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "y_scores = sgd_clf.decision_function([digit])\n",
        "\n",
        "threshold = 0\n",
        "\n",
        "y_digit_pred = (y_scores > threshold) # Gives same result as normal predict\n",
        "\n",
        "threshold = 3000\n",
        "\n",
        "y_digit_pred = (y_scores > threshold)"
      ],
      "id": "583a0216",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### Using CV with Precision Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "We can use `cross_val_predict` but instead of the method being `predict`, we\n",
        "can use `decision_function`"
      ],
      "id": "0e75bda8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.metrics import precision_recall_curve\n",
        "\n",
        "y_scores = cross_val_predict(sgd_clf, X_train, y_train_5, cv=3,\n",
        "                             method=\"decision_function\")\n",
        "\n",
        "precisions, recalls, thresholds = precision_recall_curve(y_train_5, y_scores)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.plot(thresholds, precisions[:-1], \"b--\", label=\"Precision\",linewidth=2)\n",
        "plt.plot(thresholds, recalls[:-1], \"g-\", label=\"Recall\",linewidth=2)\n",
        "plt.vlines(threshold, 0,1.0, \"k\", \"dotted\", label=\"threshold\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "00ad0465",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.plot(recalls, precisions, linewidth=2,label=\"Precision/Recall Curve\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "39d5aa59",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Specifying a precision or recall\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we want to attain a 90% precision or recall, we could either search\n",
        "the graph for that point or use NumPy'a `argmax()` function which returns the\n",
        "index of that maximum value"
      ],
      "id": "96445c99"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "idx_for_90_precision = (precision_score >= 0.90).argmax()\n",
        "threshold_for_90_precision = thresholds[idx_for_90_precision]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(threshold_for_90_precision)\n",
        "\n",
        "y_train_pred_90 = (y_scores >= threshold_for_90_precision)\n",
        "print(precision_score(y_train_5, y_train_pred_90))\n",
        "print(recall_score(y_train_5, y_train_pred_90))"
      ],
      "id": "60e0c836",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## The ROC Curve\n"
      ],
      "id": "219680ce"
    }
  ],
